{"causes": ["The ongoing collapse of the Eastern Han dynasty created a power vacuum that allowed ambitious warlords like Yuan Shao to emerge and seek regional dominance.", "Yuan Shao's strategic ambition to unify northern China necessitated a large army and a robust logistical network, placing Chunyu Qiong in a critical command role.", "The strategic importance of Wuchao as the primary supply depot for Yuan Shao's massive army made its defense, and thus Chunyu Qiong's command, central to the Battle of Guandu."], "effects": ["Chunyu Qiong's defeat and the destruction of Wuchao critically undermined Yuan Shao's logistical capabilities and severely damaged his army's morale.", "The fall of Wuchao directly contributed to Cao Cao's decisive victory over Yuan Shao at the Battle of Guandu, altering the course of Chinese history.", "His capture and subsequent execution solidified Cao Cao's dominance in northern China, paving the way for the establishment of the Cao Wei state during the Three Kingdoms period."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "chunyu-qiong", "sourceName": "Chunyu Qiong", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-guandu", "targetName": "Battle of Guandu", "context": "Chunyu Qiong commanded Yuan Shao's critical supply depot at Wuchao during the decisive Battle of Guandu in 200 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "cao-cao", "sourceName": "Cao Cao", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "chunyu-qiong", "targetName": "Chunyu Qiong", "context": "Cao Cao's audacious night raid on Wuchao directly led to Chunyu Qiong's defeat and capture in 200 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "chunyu-qiong", "sourceName": "Chunyu Qiong", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "collapse-of-eastern-han-dynasty", "targetName": "Collapse of Eastern Han Dynasty", "context": "Chunyu Qiong's military career unfolded during the tumultuous final decades of the Eastern Han dynasty, marked by widespread warlordism."}, {"sourceSlug": "wuchao", "sourceName": "Wuchao", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "hebei-province-china", "targetName": "Hebei Province, China", "context": "The strategic supply depot of Wuchao, commanded by Chunyu Qiong, was located in what is now Hebei Province, China."}, {"sourceSlug": "yuan-shao", "sourceName": "Yuan Shao", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "chunyu-qiong", "targetName": "Chunyu Qiong", "context": "Chunyu Qiong served as a key military commander and administrator under the warlord Yuan Shao during his campaigns for northern Chinese supremacy."}], "places": [{"name": "Wuchao, China", "role": "Strategic Supply Depot"}, {"name": "Hebei Province, China", "role": "Region of Conflict"}, {"name": "Yecheng, China", "role": "Yuan Shao's Base"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Chunyu Qiong", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "chunyu-qiong"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1089642", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chunyu_Qiong"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Chunyu Qiong's defeat at Wuchao was a decisive turning point in the Battle of Guandu, directly leading to Yuan Shao's collapse and Cao Cao's consolidation of northern China, profoundly shaping the trajectory towards the Three Kingdoms period.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-14T08:17:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43574567599", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Chunyu Qiong (b. 200 CE, China) was a public servant or civil administrator of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in China. Known in the fields of military leaders & commanders.", "newValue": "Chunyu Qiong (d. 200 CE) was a pivotal military commander and administrator serving under the powerful warlord Yuan Shao during the twilight years of the Eastern Han dynasty. His career, situated within the tumultuous period that prefaced the Three Kingdoms, was inextricably linked to the struggle for supremacy in northern China. Specifically, Chunyu Qiong's command of the crucial Wuchao supply depot became a flashpoint in the decisive Battle of Guandu in 200 CE, making him a figure whose actions, and ultimately his defeat, dramatically altered the geopolitical landscape.

During Yuan Shao's grand campaign against his rival Cao Cao, Chunyu Qiong was entrusted with the defense of Wuchao, the primary logistical hub for Yuan Shao's vast army, holding immense stores of grain and supplies. In the autumn of 200 CE, with the main forces stalemated at Guandu, Cao Cao executed a daring night raid on Wuchao with a small, elite detachment. Despite Chunyu Qiong's initial resistance, his forces were overwhelmed, the depot was set ablaze, and the commander himself was captured. This audacious maneuver by Cao Cao was a catastrophic blow to Yuan Shao's war effort, severely impacting his army's morale and logistical capacity.

The fall of Wuchao and Chunyu Qiong's subsequent capture and execution directly precipitated Yuan Shao's complete rout at Guandu. This military disaster effectively broke Yuan Shao's power, allowing Cao Cao to consolidate his control over northern China and lay the groundwork for the future Cao Wei state. Chunyu Qiong's role, though ultimately one of defeat, highlights the critical importance of logistics in ancient warfare and the profound impact a single strategic point, and its commander, could have on the outcome of an entire campaign and, by extension, the course of Chinese history. His story serves as a stark reminder of the high stakes and brutal realities of warlord conflicts during the Classical era."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-14T08:17:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43574567599", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "200 CE, China"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "200 CE, China"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing collapse of the Eastern Han dynasty created a power vacuum that allowed ambitious warlords like Yuan Shao to emerge and seek regional dominance.", "Yuan Shao's strategic ambition to unify northern China necessitated a large army and a robust logistical network, placing Chunyu Qiong in a critical command role.", "The strategic importance of Wuchao as the primary supply depot for Yuan Shao's massive army made its defense, and thus Chunyu Qiong's command, central to the Battle of Guandu."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-14T08:17:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43574567599", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "200 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "M